2026-04-22T17:45:39Z	CH.X.J.001	The answer states that 'applying aft cyclic while the rotor is unloaded is the primary control input that causes mast bumping' and then states 'the correct recovery from low-G is to apply gentle right lateral cyclic... to reload the rotor before any aft cyclic input.' This is contradictory and factually incorrect based on FAA-H-8083-21B. The correct low-G recovery procedure is to apply aft cyclic (not lateral/right cyclic) to reload the rotor. Lateral cyclic during low-G is what causes mast bumping.; The correct recovery from low-G is to apply gentle AFT cyclic (to increase rotor thrust and restore positive G loading), NOT right lateral cyclic. Lateral cyclic during low-G is the dangerous input that can trigger mast bumping. Revise the answer to state: 'The correct recovery from low-G is to apply gentle aft cyclic to reload the rotor and restore positive G. Lateral cyclic inputs during low-G conditions are what create the risk of mast bumping.'
2026-04-22T17:45:39Z	CH.X.J.002	This answer contains a critical factual error. It states 'Applying aft cyclic while the rotor is unloaded (low-G condition) is the primary control input that causes mast bumping' and then contradicts itself by stating 'The correct recovery from low-G is to apply gentle right lateral cyclic... to reload the rotor before any aft cyclic input.' This is backwards. Per FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 11, aft cyclic is the correct recovery input for low-G. It is LATERAL cyclic that causes mast bumping during low-G conditions.; The answer has the recovery procedure completely reversed. Correct version: 'LATERAL cyclic input (not aft cyclic) while the rotor is unloaded is the primary control input that causes mast bumping. The correct recovery from low-G is to apply gentle aft cyclic to reload the rotor and restore positive G loading. Lateral cyclic inputs should be avoided during the initial low-G recovery phase.'
2026-04-22T17:45:39Z	CH.X.J.004	The answer states 'The correct recovery is to first apply smooth, gentle right lateral cyclic... to reload the rotor disk and re-establish positive G loading. Once the rotor is reloaded... you may then use gentle aft cyclic.' This is factually incorrect according to FAA-H-8083-21B Chapter 11. The correct recovery procedure is to apply AFT cyclic first to reload the rotor, not lateral cyclic. Lateral cyclic during low-G is the dangerous input that can cause mast bumping. The sequence in this answer is backwards.; The correct low-G recovery procedure is: Apply gentle AFT cyclic first to increase rotor thrust and restore positive G loading. Once positive G is re-established, then lateral cyclic inputs can be made if needed to correct attitude. Revise to: 'The correct recovery is to first apply smooth, gentle aft cyclic to reload the rotor and restore positive G loading. Once positive G is re-established and you feel normal seat pressure, then you may use gentle lateral cyclic or other inputs to recover the nose-down attitude. Avoid lateral cyclic inputs during the initial low-G phase, as these can cause mast bumping.'
